---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ShelfTag from '../components/ShelfTag.astro';
import { states, storeInfo } from '../lib/data';
const sts = states();
---
<Base
  title="About and how prices are checked"
  description="Who runs Grams per Dollar, what the yellow protein-per-dollar tag means, and how recipe costs and nutrition are worked out."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About Grams per Dollar</h1>
    <p>
      Grams per Dollar is a small, independent site about high-protein meal prep on a budget,
      run by two people who cook most of what they eat at home. Many of the recipes are written and photographed by Sanae.
    </p>
    <p>
      We're into nutrition, and we hate overpaying for food. So we cook to get as much protein as
      we can out of every dollar. The meals here are the ones that pass that test: filling,
      high in protein, and cheap enough that you won't be tempted by takeout or pricey meal kits.
    </p>

    <h2>What the yellow tag means</h2>
    <p class="tag-row">
      <ShelfTag value={27.6} />
      <span>
        It's grams of protein you get for each dollar spent on a recipe's ingredients. A meal with
        50 g of protein that costs $2.00 scores 25. Higher is better value.
      </span>
    </p>

    <h2>How prices are checked</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Prices come from US grocery chains' own websites, for one named store each. Right now that's:
        <ul>
          {sts.map((st) => st.ids.map((id) => <li>{storeInfo(id).store}: {storeInfo(id).location}, {st.state} ({storeInfo(id).source})</li>))}
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>We use the regular price, not sales, and each store's own everyday brand where it has one.</li>
      <li>We only compare stores within the same state, because prices differ from city to city.</li>
      <li>Each recipe is shown at the store where it gives the most protein per dollar, with what it costs at every other store underneath.</li>
      <li>We count only the amount a recipe uses, not the whole pack. A teaspoon of cumin costs a few cents, not the price of the jar.</li>
      <li>Every recipe shows the month its prices were checked. We recheck them every few months.</li>
      <li>Prices vary a lot between states and stores, so treat them as a guide to which meals are good value, not an exact bill.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where the nutrition numbers come from</h2>
    <p>
      Protein and calories come from <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/">USDA FoodData Central</a>,
      using raw weights. Branded products like protein powder use the product's own label, because
      generic entries don't match real products well.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is not</h2>
    <p>
      This site shares recipes, shopping lists and costs. It doesn't give medical, weight-loss
      or supplement advice. If you have a health condition, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .tag-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: 1rem; align-items: center; }
  @media (max-width: 26rem) { .tag-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
